1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:01,090
You're a programmer.

2
00:00:01,090 --> 00:00:02,773
This one line is the ultimate command

3
00:00:02,773 --> 00:00:04,139
that solves all work troubles.

4
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:07,350
It'll wipe all system files, almost unrecoverable.

5
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:09,070
Run it, and poof—work's gone,

6
00:00:09,070 --> 00:00:10,298
so are your troubles.

7
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:14,806
So, goal achieved, right? You'd never manually run this.

8
00:00:14,806 --> 00:00:18,949
But an AI Agent might, since it's essentially a loop.

9
00:00:19,270 --> 00:00:20,581
It assembles the context,

10
00:00:20,581 --> 00:00:23,150
sends it to the large language model to reason.

11
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:26,206
The AI Agent runs tools based on the model's response,

12
00:00:26,206 --> 00:00:28,180
feeds the results back into context,

13
00:00:28,180 --> 00:00:30,100
then loops to reason and act again.

14
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:31,170
If this loop runs too often,

15
00:00:31,210 --> 00:00:34,170
The context gets too long, causing truncation.

16
00:00:34,410 --> 00:00:35,826
The AI Agent's objective

17
00:00:35,826 --> 00:00:38,246
could drift, executing dangerous commands,

18
00:00:38,246 --> 00:00:40,490
such as the deletion shown previously.

19
00:00:40,710 --> 00:00:43,672
To tackle this, AI Agents introduce sandboxes.

20
00:00:43,672 --> 00:00:45,580
But what exactly is a sandbox,

21
00:00:45,580 --> 00:00:49,069
and how does it differ from Docker containers or VMs?

22
00:00:49,260 --> 00:00:51,571
Today, we'll tie all these concepts together.

23
00:00:51,571 --> 00:00:52,621
Then, let's check out

24
00:00:52,621 --> 00:00:54,115
the cutting edge open source

25
00:00:54,115 --> 00:00:56,037
Cloud Sandbox solution, CubeSandbox.

26
00:00:56,037 --> 00:00:57,928
So, have you hit that like button yet?

27
00:00:57,930 --> 00:00:58,850
followed? Thanks!

28
00:00:59,810 --> 00:01:02,691
So, what's a sandbox? Reminds me of a joke from childhood:

29
00:01:02,691 --> 00:01:05,730
Where's the safest place for a pimple? Someone else's face.

30
00:01:05,970 --> 00:01:08,528
Same logic. Running commands with an Agent is risky,

31
00:01:08,528 --> 00:01:10,660
but as long as it's not in *my* environment,

32
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:11,700
it's basically safe.

33
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:13,220
This specially designated,

34
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:15,687
isolated environment for high risk operations

35
00:01:15,687 --> 00:01:16,760
is called a sandbox.

36
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:18,773
Wrap risky cmds in an app,

37
00:01:18,773 --> 00:01:20,062
run it in the sandbox.

38
00:01:20,062 --> 00:01:21,175
Even if it crashes,

39
00:01:21,175 --> 00:01:23,167
the AI Agent's env stays untouched.

40
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:25,704
Sandboxes can run locally or in the cloud.

41
00:01:25,704 --> 00:01:27,696
The latter is called a Cloud Sandbox,

42
00:01:27,696 --> 00:01:28,706
and it's currently

43
00:01:28,706 --> 00:01:31,459
the most common isolation solution for AI Agents.

44
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:32,837
So, that begs the question.

45
00:01:32,837 --> 00:01:35,329
This sounds a lot like a cloud Virtual Machine .

46
00:01:35,329 --> 00:01:35,899
What's diff?

47
00:01:36,730 --> 00:01:39,899
We know the difference between a sandbox, VM, and container.

48
00:01:39,899 --> 00:01:41,757
The computers we can see and touch

49
00:01:41,757 --> 00:01:43,342
are called physical machines,

50
00:01:43,342 --> 00:01:45,419
or Bare Metal Server on Tencent Cloud.

51
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:48,466
A Bare Metal Server has plenty of CPU and memory.

52
00:01:48,466 --> 00:01:50,301
Using software like VMware or KVM,

53
00:01:50,301 --> 00:01:52,104
you can slice up those resources

54
00:01:52,104 --> 00:01:54,529
to create multiple virtual machines, or VMs.

55
00:01:54,930 --> 00:01:57,570
Each Virtual Machine has its own OS and dedicated resources.

56
00:01:57,890 --> 00:01:59,843
VMs are completely isolated.

57
00:01:59,843 --> 00:02:02,843
Any internal damage won't affect other VMs,

58
00:02:02,843 --> 00:02:04,378
ensuring high security.

59
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:06,643
But the thing is, it has a full OS kernel,

60
00:02:06,643 --> 00:02:08,758
which is bulky and boots up way too slow.

61
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:10,229
Use that for a sandbox?

62
00:02:10,229 --> 00:02:11,814
If an AI Agent needs to run

63
00:02:11,814 --> 00:02:14,867
code, spawning a temp Virtual Machine takes forever.

64
00:02:14,867 --> 00:02:15,219
No way.

65
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:16,380
Speed it up?

66
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:18,652
Yes. Since the OS kernel's too heavy,

67
00:02:18,652 --> 00:02:19,729
let's switch gears.

68
00:02:19,729 --> 00:02:22,423
Build on Virtual Machine tech, packaging apps,

69
00:02:22,423 --> 00:02:24,219
deps, and configs in isolation.

70
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:26,002
Limit its max memory and CPU,

71
00:02:26,002 --> 00:02:27,681
creating isolated environments,

72
00:02:27,681 --> 00:02:29,640
which are what we call 'containers'.

73
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:31,779
A container is essentially a process,

74
00:02:31,779 --> 00:02:34,399
so it boots fast, meeting sandbox performance needs.

75
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,998
So, early on, many vendors would build on containers,

76
00:02:36,998 --> 00:02:38,732
adding stricter permission controls

77
00:02:38,732 --> 00:02:40,120
for Cloud Sandbox solutions.

78
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:41,235
But the problem is,

79
00:02:41,235 --> 00:02:44,271
multiple containers share the same Host Machine kernel.

80
00:02:44,271 --> 00:02:46,601
If there's a kernel vulnerability or escape,

81
00:02:46,601 --> 00:02:48,499
the impact could be the whole host.

82
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:50,540
Taking down other containers.

83
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:52,540
So it's only for low risk operations.

84
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:53,435
We can't guarantee

85
00:02:53,435 --> 00:02:55,674
an AI Agent won't execute high risk commands,

86
00:02:55,674 --> 00:02:56,719
ones that could harm.

87
00:02:56,960 --> 00:03:00,126
So the industry streamlined traditional Virtual Machine s,

88
00:03:00,126 --> 00:03:02,460
trimming modules for device compatibility

89
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:05,460
and keeping only the virtual hardware apps truly need.

90
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:08,599
Creating a Virtual Machine with a minimalist OS kernel,

91
00:03:08,599 --> 00:03:09,500
known as MicroVM.

92
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:11,813
Thus, with an independent OS kernel

93
00:03:11,813 --> 00:03:13,830
providing a strong security boundary

94
00:03:13,830 --> 00:03:16,448
and much faster boot times than traditional VMs,

95
00:03:16,448 --> 00:03:19,120
the MicroVM based Cloud Sandbox approach emerged.

96
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:21,672
But at its core, it's still a Virtual Machine .

97
00:03:21,672 --> 00:03:23,638
To hit that millisecond response time

98
00:03:23,638 --> 00:03:25,499
AI Agents need, more tweaks are key.

99
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:27,718
Next, let's take CubeSandbox as an example

100
00:03:27,718 --> 00:03:30,180
and see how cutting edge industry solutions do it.

101
00:03:30,730 --> 00:03:32,170
What is CubeSandbox?

102
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:34,090
CubeSandbox is a skyrocketing

103
00:03:34,090 --> 00:03:36,126
open source Cloud Sandbox on GitHub.

104
00:03:36,126 --> 00:03:37,710
Developed by Tencent Cloud,

105
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:41,047
you can basically think of it as a cloud sandbox foundation

106
00:03:41,047 --> 00:03:42,179
built for AI Agents.

107
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:45,707
It handles sandbox creation, isolation, and cleanup,

108
00:03:45,707 --> 00:03:49,550
deploying quickly on your hardware for Cloud Sandbox service.

109
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:52,687
When the Agent needs to run code, commands, or file ops,

110
00:03:52,687 --> 00:03:54,171
it calls CubeSandbox's API,

111
00:03:54,171 --> 00:03:57,140
which spins up a temporary Cloud Sandbox to execute.

112
00:03:57,440 --> 00:04:00,280
It's reusable multiple times and then destroyed.

113
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:02,938
It does the same thing as another open source

114
00:04:02,938 --> 00:04:04,052
Cloud Sandbox solution,

115
00:04:04,052 --> 00:04:04,440
E two B.

116
00:04:04,850 --> 00:04:06,253
But what's different is,

117
00:04:06,253 --> 00:04:09,730
CubeSandbox is better open sourced and cheaper to set up.

118
00:04:10,010 --> 00:04:12,320
Yuanbao AI coding assistant's online programming,

119
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:15,055
and Minimax's latest large language model Agent training,

120
00:04:15,055 --> 00:04:15,690
all use this.

121
00:04:16,010 --> 00:04:17,611
Used to be for big companies,

122
00:04:17,611 --> 00:04:19,928
but now even regular developers can use it.

123
00:04:20,810 --> 00:04:22,344
CubeSandbox Architecture:

124
00:04:22,344 --> 00:04:23,264
To achieve this,

125
00:04:23,264 --> 00:04:25,904
CubeSandbox divides servers into two types:

126
00:04:25,904 --> 00:04:28,299
the control plane and the worker nodes.

127
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:31,700
Simply put, their relationship is boss and the workers.

128
00:04:31,980 --> 00:04:34,360
The control plane manages all Nodes,

129
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:36,289
typically bare metal servers

130
00:04:36,289 --> 00:04:39,597
responsible for creating and running sandboxes.

131
00:04:39,597 --> 00:04:41,909
It includes CubeAPI and CubeMaster.

132
00:04:42,170 --> 00:04:44,691
CubeAPI provides E two B compatible interfaces,

133
00:04:44,691 --> 00:04:46,529
so E two B apps can be integrated

134
00:04:46,529 --> 00:04:48,090
just by changing an address.

135
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:49,965
After CubeAPI gets the request,

136
00:04:49,965 --> 00:04:52,640
CubeMaster sends a msg for Node to create sandbox.

137
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:53,808
Inside the Node,

138
00:04:53,808 --> 00:04:56,719
a Cubelet component receives commands from CubeMaster.

139
00:04:56,920 --> 00:04:59,175
Upon receiving the create sandbox command,

140
00:04:59,175 --> 00:05:01,320
Cubelet drives KVM to create a MicroVM.

141
00:05:01,320 --> 00:05:04,491
Starting a sandbox from scratch, one MicroVM is too slow,

142
00:05:04,491 --> 00:05:07,039
so CubeSandbox pre launches one ahead of time.

143
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:10,404
Run the slow stuff like system env, deps, and biz boot up,

144
00:05:10,404 --> 00:05:13,000
then save that state as a template snapshot.

145
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:14,830
So when new requests come in,

146
00:05:14,830 --> 00:05:17,560
it clones a new MicroVM directly from this snapshot.

147
00:05:17,870 --> 00:05:19,747
The slow initialization is done upfront

148
00:05:19,747 --> 00:05:20,951
when making the template,

149
00:05:20,951 --> 00:05:23,310
which is why startup latency is under tens of ms.

150
00:05:23,460 --> 00:05:26,060
Once the VM boots, the user's service is ready to go.

151
00:05:26,260 --> 00:05:28,300
Initially, each sandbox's memory and disk

152
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:30,340
point to the very same underlying pages.

153
00:05:30,570 --> 00:05:32,593
Copies are made only when written

154
00:05:32,593 --> 00:05:36,210
to, keeping each sandbox's memory overhead to just a few MB.

155
00:05:36,550 --> 00:05:39,670
CubeSandbox completely abstracts away these details.

156
00:05:39,990 --> 00:05:44,198
AI Agents just send code and commands via CubeSandbox's API

157
00:05:44,198 --> 00:05:45,696
to a Node's sandbox,

158
00:05:45,696 --> 00:05:47,479
ensuring maximum security.

159
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:51,680
Got the architecture, let's get hands on.

160
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,419
First, provisioning servers, deploying CubeSandbox,

161
00:05:54,419 --> 00:05:57,495
and all that. I've prepared a detailed doc with steps for you,

162
00:05:57,495 --> 00:05:59,310
so just follow along and you're set.

163
00:05:59,550 --> 00:06:01,866
Once that's set, we can use this demo code

164
00:06:01,866 --> 00:06:04,749
to send the AI Agent's task to CubeSandbox to run.

165
00:06:04,749 --> 00:06:07,630
CubeSandbox will then return the results back to us

166
00:06:07,630 --> 00:06:09,269
through the 'result' parameter.

167
00:06:09,470 --> 00:06:11,242
Next, let's run a quick stress test

168
00:06:11,242 --> 00:06:14,110
to see how the sandbox performs under high concurrency.

169
00:06:14,570 --> 00:06:16,446
Under fifty concurrent stress tests,

170
00:06:16,446 --> 00:06:19,203
CubeSandbox achieves P ninety nine sandbox creation

171
00:06:19,203 --> 00:06:21,474
around one hundred and fifty milliseconds,

172
00:06:21,474 --> 00:06:22,880
meeting performance specs.

173
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:25,235
If you're interested in this project,

174
00:06:25,235 --> 00:06:26,398
feel free to star it.

175
00:06:26,398 --> 00:06:28,783
In the past, when talking about AI Agents,

176
00:06:28,783 --> 00:06:31,460
the focus was on large language model , prompts,

177
00:06:31,620 --> 00:06:34,539
But now, with concepts like Harness engineering rising,

178
00:06:34,539 --> 00:06:36,050
people are focusing more on

179
00:06:36,050 --> 00:06:38,009
the Agent's execution environment.

180
00:06:38,009 --> 00:06:39,991
An execution Agent without a sandbox

181
00:06:39,991 --> 00:06:41,919
is basically experimenting in prod.

182
00:06:42,100 --> 00:06:43,456
Only by caging risk

183
00:06:43,456 --> 00:06:47,310
can we confidently deploy AI Agents for real business,

184
00:06:47,310 --> 00:06:50,380
truly freeing people from repetitive tasks.

